# ActiveRecord::Relation — generic signature bundled by
# rigor-activerecord (ADR-25 plugin-contributed RBS).
#
# Purpose: give `has_many` / `has_and_belongs_to_many` accessors,
# `Model.where(...)` / `Model.all` / `Model.order(...)` and
# user-declared `scope`s a precise *element* type. The plugin
# contributes `ActiveRecord::Relation[Model]` for those call
# sites; this file is what makes the chained relation methods
# (`.where`, `.order`, `.first`, `.each { |m| ... }`, …) resolve.
#
# This is NOT a faithful transcription of Rails' Relation API —
# it is the surface Rigor needs to type-check idiomatic relation
# usage WITHOUT false positives. Two design rules:
#
# - Chain methods return `self`, so `Relation[Model]` survives an
#   arbitrarily long query chain.
# - The method list is deliberately generous. A method that is
#   genuinely on `ActiveRecord::Relation` but missing here would
#   surface as a false `call.undefined-method` once the plugin
#   has typed the receiver — so the list errs toward inclusion.
#   `include Enumerable[Elem]` carries the whole iteration
#   surface; the class body only adds relation-specific methods
#   and the ones whose argument shape differs from Enumerable's.
#
# If the project supplies its own ActiveRecord RBS (e.g. via
# `rbs collection`), that definition conflicts with this one and
# the conflict degrades through Rigor's plugin-RBS failure memo
# (ADR-25 WD4) — the richer upstream definition simply wins.
#
# ## Effect annotations (ADR-103 WD10, issue #387)
#
# Every method carries an effect annotation, and the split down
# the middle of this file is the whole point of the Rails effect
# layer: a **query builder** is `%a{pure}` because it issues nothing —
# `where` composes an Arel tree and returns a relation — while a
# **materializer** is `%a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}` because it
# is the call that finally runs the query. A presenter that builds
# and returns a scope has pure code; the caller that materialises
# it gets the read. That is the truthful reading of what the code
# does, not of how Rails developers narrate it.
#
# This is the plugin's tier-1 channel: these are *accepted
# signatures*, so a call site importing one of these bounds is
# exhaustive rather than tainted (ADR-103 WD6). The methods this
# file does not declare — `map`, `filter_map` and the rest of the
# `Enumerable` surface, which materialise by delegating to `each`
# — are covered by the plugin's `effect_attributions:` instead,
# because declaring them here would change how they TYPE.
#
# ## Every bound here also bounds the association's relations
#
# A `has_many` reader returns an
# `ActiveRecord::Associations::CollectionProxy`, and a query
# builder called on the proxy (`user.posts.where(…)`) returns an
# `ActiveRecord::AssociationRelation`. Both are Relation subclasses
# that still hold the association, and the plugin types both as
# `Relation[Model]`. A call on either imports this file's bound, so
# each annotation has to hold for all three classes. The facts below
# are checked against activerecord 8.1.3.1.
#
# Where one of them adds records to the association's in-memory
# target or discards them from it, the bound carries `mutate.self`:
#
# - `build` / `new`, `create` / `create!` and the `find_or_*`,
#   `create_or_find_by` and `first_or_*` builders reach
#   `@association.build` / `create`, which call `add_to_target`. On
#   the proxy they are overrides. On an AssociationRelation they are
#   its private `_new` / `_create`.
# - `reset`, `reload`, `delete_all` and `destroy_all` on the proxy
#   reset the association, which drops every record built there and
#   not yet saved. So do `update_all` and `touch_all`: the proxy does
#   not override them, and Relation's version ends with
#   `.tap { reset }`, which reaches the proxy's `reset`.
# - `insert_all`, `insert_all!` and `upsert_all` on the proxy reset
#   the target only when it holds no unsaved record. Rails 8.1
#   deprecates the other branch and says those records will be lost
#   in 8.2. The bound is written for 8.2, and it over-states 8.1.
#
# So `user.posts.build` changes an object the caller can still reach
# through `user`: `user.posts.size` counts the new record, and
# `user.save` saves it. On a plain Relation, `new` and `build` change
# nothing, so `mutate.self` over-states them there. The type cannot
# tell the receivers apart, and an upper bound may be too wide but
# never too narrow.
#
# Loading is not counted as a change. `to_a` and `load` fill the
# target with what the database holds, which is the `io.db.read` they
# already carry.
#
# `mutate.self` is written from the callee's side, as any envelope
# is: the receiver changes itself. A caller imports the label
# unchanged, so in the caller's row it names the proxy it called,
# not the caller's own `self`.
#
# The writers a plain Relation does not define (`<<`, `push`,
# `append`, `concat`, `replace`, `clear`) are rows in the plugin's
# `effect_attributions:` rather than declarations here. Declaring
# them here would claim a Relation defines them, and would change
# how they type. The same rows cover `delete` / `destroy`. A plain
# Relation does define those, as `where(id:).delete_all` and
# `find(id).destroy`, and the rows' labels cover both. They are not
# declared here because that too would change how they type.
#
# ## A writer that also queries carries the read too
#
# `io.db.write` does not include `io.db.read`: they are sibling
# leaves. So a writer that queries before its write, or after a failed
# one, names both. In activerecord 8.1.3.1:
#
# - `find_or_create_by(!)` is `find_by || create_or_find_by`, and
#   `first_or_create(!)` is `first || create`.
# - `create_or_find_by(!)` rescues `RecordNotUnique` with `find_by!`.
# - `destroy_all` is `records.each(&:destroy)`, which loads first, and
#   `destroy_by` is `where(…).destroy_all`. `update(!)` walks `each`,
#   or passes an id to the model's `update`, which calls `find`.
# - `update_all` and `delete_all`, and so `touch_all` and `delete_by`,
#   first `SELECT` the distinct primary keys when the relation
#   eager-loads, has a limit or offset and no `group`, and eager-loads
#   or joins a collection association (`apply_join_dependency`). The
#   proxy's `delete_all` on a `has_many :through` loads the target
#   before it deletes.
# - `create(!)` on the proxy of a `has_many :through` a `has_one`
#   builds the join record through the `has_one`, which loads the
#   record it replaces.
#
# Two kinds of query are not counted. Schema reflection is one: the
# column and index lookups the schema cache serves would otherwise
# make `where` a read. The model's callbacks and validators are the
# other, including those an association option such as `dependent:`
# or `touch:` registers. They are not part of any bound here. The
# plugin's callback edge carries only symbol-argument callback macros
# and a uniqueness validator, and only on the model's own `save` /
# `destroy`. Nothing carries the reads a required `belongs_to`,
# `touch:` or `dependent:` registers.
#
# One case is not covered. On that same `has_many :through` a
# `has_one`, `new` / `build` also build the join record when the
# source reflection has an inverse, and so do `find_or_initialize_by`
# and `first_or_initialize`, which reach `new`. The `has_one` then
# replaces its record: it loads the old one, and even without a
# save it can delete, destroy or save it.

module ActiveRecord
  class Relation[Elem]
    include Enumerable[Elem]

    # `Enumerable` only *requires* `each` (the includer supplies
    # it); it is declared here so `relation.each { |m| ... }`
    # resolves and the block parameter carries the element type.
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def each: () { (Elem) -> void } -> self
            | () -> Enumerator[Elem, self]

    # --- query builders (return another relation) ---
    %a{pure}
    def where: (*untyped) ?{ () -> untyped } -> self
    %a{pure}
    def rewhere: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def not: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def or: (untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def and: (untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def order: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def reorder: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def in_order_of: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def limit: (untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def offset: (untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def distinct: (?untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def group: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def regroup: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def having: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def includes: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def preload: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def eager_load: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def joins: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def left_joins: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def left_outer_joins: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def references: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def select: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def reselect: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def unscope: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def merge: (untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def except: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def only: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def extending: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def from: (untyped, ?untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def lock: (?untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def readonly: (?untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def strict_loading: (?untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def create_with: (untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def none: () -> self
    %a{pure}
    def all: () -> self
    %a{pure}
    def unscoped: () -> self
    %a{pure}
    def default_scoped: () -> self
    %a{pure}
    def annotate: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def optimizer_hints: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def excluding: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def without: (*untyped) -> self
    %a{pure}
    def with: (*untyped) -> self
    def scoping: () { () -> untyped } -> untyped

    # --- finders (return an element) ---
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def find: (*untyped) -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def find_by: (*untyped) -> Elem?
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def find_by!: (*untyped) -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def find_sole_by: (*untyped) -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def sole: () -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def first: () -> Elem?
            | (Integer limit) -> Array[Elem]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def first!: () -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def last: () -> Elem?
           | (Integer limit) -> Array[Elem]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def last!: () -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def take: () -> Elem?
           | (Integer limit) -> Array[Elem]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def take!: () -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def second: () -> Elem?
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def third: () -> Elem?
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def fourth: () -> Elem?
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def fifth: () -> Elem?
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def forty_two: () -> Elem?
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def second_to_last: () -> Elem?
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def third_to_last: () -> Elem?

    # --- batched enumeration ---
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def find_each: (**untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def find_in_batches: (**untyped) ?{ (Array[Elem]) -> void } -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def in_batches: (**untyped) ?{ (ActiveRecord::Relation[Elem]) -> void } -> untyped

    # --- scalars / aggregates ---
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def count: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> untyped } -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def sum: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> untyped } -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def average: (untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def minimum: (untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def maximum: (untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def calculate: (*untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def pluck: (*untyped) -> Array[untyped]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def pick: (*untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def ids: () -> Array[untyped]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def size: () -> Integer
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def length: () -> Integer
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def empty?: () -> bool
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def any?: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> boolish } -> bool
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def none?: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> boolish } -> bool
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def one?: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> boolish } -> bool
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def many?: () ?{ (Elem) -> boolish } -> bool
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def exists?: (*untyped) -> bool
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def include?: (untyped) -> bool
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def member?: (untyped) -> bool

    # --- conversion / state ---
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def to_a: () -> Array[Elem]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def to_ary: () -> Array[Elem]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def entries: () -> Array[Elem]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def load: (?untyped) -> self
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, mutate.self}
    def reload: () -> self
    %a{rigor:v1:effect mutate.self}
    def reset: () -> self
    %a{pure}
    def loaded?: () -> bool
    %a{pure}
    def to_sql: () -> String
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def explain: (*untyped) -> String
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def cache_key: (?untyped) -> String
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def cache_version: (?untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def to_json: (*untyped) -> String
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def as_json: (?untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def blank?: () -> bool
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read}
    def present?: () -> bool
    %a{pure}
    def klass: () -> untyped
    %a{pure}
    def model: () -> untyped
    %a{pure}
    def table: () -> untyped
    %a{pure}
    def arel: () -> untyped

    # --- whole-relation persistence ---
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def update_all: (untyped) -> Integer
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def delete_all: () -> Integer
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def destroy_all: () -> Array[Elem]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write}
    def update: (*untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write}
    def update!: (*untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write}
    def delete_by: (*untyped) -> Integer
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write}
    def destroy_by: (*untyped) -> Array[Elem]
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def touch_all: (*untyped) -> Integer
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def insert_all: (untyped, **untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def insert_all!: (untyped, **untyped) -> untyped
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def upsert_all: (untyped, **untyped) -> untyped

    # --- instantiating builders (return an element) ---
    %a{rigor:v1:effect mutate.self}
    def new: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect mutate.self}
    def build: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def create: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def create!: (*untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def find_or_create_by: (untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def find_or_create_by!: (untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, mutate.self}
    def find_or_initialize_by: (untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def create_or_find_by: (untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def create_or_find_by!: (untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def first_or_create: (?untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, io.db.write, mutate.self}
    def first_or_create!: (?untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
    %a{rigor:v1:effect io.db.read, mutate.self}
    def first_or_initialize: (?untyped) ?{ (Elem) -> void } -> Elem
  end
end
